#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z)\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k}$, express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + z) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2}y) \\\\\n&= 1 + z + 0 \\\\\n&= z + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∭_(R)z + 1dV &= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y}^{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}z + 1dzdydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448" :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}$. Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S$. Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∭_(R)3dV \\\\\n&= 3∭_(R)dV \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot Volume(R) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}π \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 32π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133412 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=1$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k}$, find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R$, then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(e^{z}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4x^{2}yz) \\\\\n&= 0 + 2x^{2}y + 4x^{2}y \\\\\n&= 6x^{2}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S$. Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∭_(R)6x^{2}ydV \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{1}^{2}6x^{2}ydzdydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}3x^{2}dx \\cdot ∫_{0}^{2}2ydy \\cdot ∫_{1}^{2}dz \\\\\n&= [x^{3}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1} \\cdot [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2} \\cdot (2 - 1) \\\\\n&= (1 - 0) \\cdot (4 - 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1c80604-1076-58ec-ad2a-dc892c2cd559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{-2}^{4}z + 3 ydxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130912} {:answer/id #uuid "e71e3a33-1c58-5617-99f9-4d409a9d4abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{4 - y 2 - z 2}^{y - 2 z}2 y + 1dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130913} {:answer/id #uuid "4c556c37-07ca-5cd5-b6be-f45703bfa74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y - 2 z}^{4 - y 2 - z 2}y 2dxdzdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130914} 